 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite database inside this node. Each validated snapshot is rebuilt into a fresh SQLite index file; lookups query the active file directly. No background service, network dependency, or external credential is involved, which satisfies the HLD tenet that all lookup complete using files on the kiosk.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Then rebuild the lookup index from scratch: parse the validated records, write them into a new SQLite database file, and activate it only after the build completes. The previous index is never patched incrementally — every snapshot yields a full rebuild, which keeps import logic simple and makes the active index a pure function of one snapshot file.
+
+Activation is a single atomic rename of the freshly built file over the active index path. A partial or failed import therefore never becomes visible to lookups, and an import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+
+## SQLite Index
+
+| Item | Choice |
+|---|---|
+| File | One SQLite database file per kiosk, replaced wholesale on each import |
+| Schema | `instruments(serial TEXT PRIMARY KEY, description TEXT NOT NULL, calibration_date TEXT, due_date TEXT)` plus a `meta` table holding the schema version and snapshot date |
+| Serial storage | Serial numbers are stored in canonical normalized form (whitespace-trimmed, ASCII uppercase); normalization happens in the registry component before the query, so SQLite matching stays an exact primary-key lookup |
+| Query | Single parameterized `SELECT` on the primary-key index; the snapshot date is read from `meta` on each result, including NotFound |
+| Scale fit | 90,000 rows and 150 MB snapshots fit comfortably in one file; an indexed exact-match lookup completes well inside the 250 ms goal on kiosk hardware |
+
+The index file is self-describing: the snapshot date lives in `meta`, so the certificates-visible snapshot date always comes from the index that actually answered the lookup, never from a separate side file that could drift.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. If the active index file is missing or cannot be opened, lookups fail with an error rather than returning records from a stale or partially readable source.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index rebuilt from each snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | The HTTPS directory makes registry lookups depend on the lab network and adds timeout handling, violating the HLD tenet that lookup complete on kiosk files; a managed search service adds recurring cost, credentials, and external connectivity. SQLite keeps the whole import-and-lookup path inside this node with no service to run, at the cost of each kiosk rebuilding its own index per snapshot — acceptable at 90,000 records. |
+| Import strategy | Full index rebuild per snapshot | Incremental patching of the previous index | A rebuild makes the active index a pure function of one snapshot file and keeps atomic activation a single rename; incremental patching would complicate failure recovery for no benefit at this record count. |
+| Serial normalization point | Normalize in the registry component; store canonical serials | SQLite case-insensitive collation | One normalization implementation, shared by import and lookup, avoids divergence between what is stored and what is queried. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Lookup backend: local embedded SQLite (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+1. Whether to verify the active index file's integrity at kiosk startup (beyond open-failure detection) or trust the atomic-rename activation.
+2. SQLite journal/WAL settings for the build step; the active file is read-only in practice.
